WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.408
Raj: Runner health looks normal across pools. CPU and disk have enough room.

2
00:00:06.408 --> 00:00:12.481
Priya: Okay, queued builds remain held safely. What's the ETA for a control?

3
00:00:12.481 --> 00:00:18.817
Lukas: Use one isolated runner for comparison. Match its image and network policy.

4
00:00:18.817 --> 00:00:25.418
Anika: Same commit on both runners, right? Otherwise we'd change two variables together.

5
00:00:25.418 --> 00:00:34.058
Hannah: Use approved short-lived test credentials there. Don't copy production secrets into diagnostics.

6
00:00:34.058 --> 00:00:40.514
Raj: Both runners fail the same fetch. Managed configuration matches the approved image.

7
00:00:40.514 --> 00:00:47.067
Lukas: That weakens a single-host failure theory. It doesn't clear shared image configuration.

8
00:00:47.067 --> 00:00:53.595
Priya: Just to confirm, don't recycle pools. We can't rule that out completely.

9
00:00:53.595 --> 00:00:59.908
Hannah: The fetch error names an endpoint. Compare that with the approved source.

10
00:00:59.908 --> 00:01:06.580
Anika: Actually, that endpoint looks rather old. Let's check dependency settings next.

